<DocPage eyebrow="USE CASE / AI" title="AI-assisted development and AI players in release builds" lead="Gua supports both AI coding agents that implement and verify a game and AI agent players that use approved information and operations to play its release build." description="Use Gua for AI-assisted Godot and Unity development and for AI players operating safely through a release build's Player profile." section="mcp">
  <h2>Two kinds of AI supported by Gua</h2>
  <div class="grid-2"><div class="mini-card"><strong>AI that assists development</strong><p>It changes code and uses Debug information, logs, and screenshots to verify the running result and fix problems.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>AI that plays a release build</strong><p>It does not change code. It participates as a player using only Player-approved UI, World information, and operations.</p></div></div>
  <h2>1. Give AI coding agents a way to verify running games</h2>
  <p>The coding agent writes the game; Gua provides the runtime UI layer it uses to check the result. Through <code>gui-mcp</code>, the agent can access the Semantic UI Tree, actions, waits, logs, screenshots, recordings, visual comparisons, and test execution.</p>
  <div class="flow"><span>AI implements</span><b>→</b><span>Launch the game</span><b>→</b><span>Inspect and operate</span><b>→</b><span>Verify and fix</span></div>
  <h2>The AI development loop</h2>
  <ol class="steps"><li><strong>Implement the feature</strong><p>The AI coding agent changes Godot GDScript/C# or Unity C# and the game UI.</p></li><li><strong>Launch the game</strong><p>The Godot GDScript add-on collects Controls. The Unity UPM package automatically collects runtime UI Toolkit, uGUI, and TextMeshPro controls. Both publish through the Gua WebSocket bridge.</p></li><li><strong>Inspect the Semantic UI Tree</strong><p>The agent calls <code>get_ui_tree</code> and identifies a control from its ID, role, text, and state without depending on engine-specific object APIs.</p></li><li><strong>Operate and wait for the result</strong><p>It sends click, value, selection, scroll, or key actions and waits for the expected UI state.</p></li><li><strong>Verify from evidence and fix</strong><p>On failure, it inspects the tree, engine logs, screenshot, or visual diff, changes the implementation, and runs the test again.</p></li></ol>
  <h2>2. Let an AI agent player play the release build</h2>
  <p>In a release build, the AI is a participant in the game rather than a test operator with developer access. The game fixes the Player profile and exposure policy that select visible UI, World Objects, and permitted UI operations. Semantic Game Actions and Raw Input are separate permissions and remain unavailable until explicitly enabled.</p>
  <div class="flow"><span>Launch release</span><b>→</b><span>Observe Player view</span><b>→</b><span>Choose a goal</span><b>→</b><span>Play with approved input</span></div>
  <div class="grid-2"><div class="mini-card"><strong>Understand the game world</strong><p>The World Object Tree describes opted-in objectives, enemies, doors, checkpoints, and other game concepts without dumping scene nodes or internal state.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>Operate the game</strong><p>Semantic UI Actions operate menus. Semantic Game Actions such as jump or move, and Raw Input only when needed, drive gameplay.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>See only the Player view</strong><p>Private objects, internal values, and Debug logs are excluded, and screenshots are denied by default. The AI cannot elevate itself to Debug.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>End input safely</strong><p>Held input is isolated by owner and returns to neutral after lease expiry, disconnect, unregister, or engine shutdown.</p></div></div>
  <h2>Development AI versus AI player</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>AI development and verification</th><th>AI player in a release build</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Goal</td><td>Verify implementation and fix problems</td><td>Participate and play as a player</td></tr><tr><td>Observation profile</td><td>Developer-managed Debug</td><td>Host-fixed Player</td></tr><tr><td>Observation</td><td>UI, logs, screenshots, and diagnostics</td><td>Approved UI and World information; no Debug logs</td></tr><tr><td>Operations</td><td>Semantic operations needed for testing and diagnosis</td><td>Approved UI operations plus separately authorized gameplay input</td></tr><tr><td>Connection</td><td>Primarily <code>gui-mcp</code> for native games</td><td>Player-configured <code>gui-mcp</code>, or <code>gua-webmcp</code> for web games</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <h2>Engine integration</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>Runtime UI source</th><th>How Gua starts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Godot 4.7</td><td>Standard <code>Control</code> tree</td><td>The GDScript add-on attaches an automatic adapter and starts the bridge.</td></tr><tr><td>Unity 6</td><td>UI Toolkit, uGUI, and TextMeshPro runtime UI</td><td>The UPM package starts automatically before scene load and runs collection after each frame.</td></tr><tr><td>Both</td><td>Semantic UI Tree v2</td><td><code>gui-mcp</code> uses the same locators, actions, waits, logs, screenshots, Visual, and Recording tools.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <h2>How it maps to Playwright on the web</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Web UI automation</th><th>Game UI automation with Gua</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Browser</td><td>Running game</td></tr><tr><td>DOM</td><td>Semantic UI Tree</td></tr><tr><td>locator</td><td>Semantic locator by ID, role, text, and state</td></tr><tr><td>click / fill / keyboard</td><td>Semantic actions such as click / set value / press key</td></tr><tr><td>expect / wait</td><td>GuaAssertions / wait_for_node</td></tr><tr><td>screenshot / visual test</td><td>Gua screenshot / visual baseline comparison</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>Can Gua test Godot UI?</h3><p>Yes. The recommended Godot 4.7 GDScript add-on reflects standard Controls and supports semantic locators, actions, waits, assertions, screenshots, and visual comparison.</p>
  <h3>Can Gua test Unity UI?</h3><p>Yes. The Unity 6 UPM package automatically reflects runtime UI Toolkit, uGUI, and TextMeshPro controls on supported Windows x64 Mono builds. The AI uses the same semantic MCP tools as it does with Godot.</p>
  <h3>Can an AI play a release build?</h3><p>Yes. Native games can use Player-configured <code>gui-mcp</code>, and web games can use <code>gua-webmcp</code>. The AI observes approved UI and World information and plays with approved UI operations and gameplay input. Development Debug information and Player permissions remain separate.</p>
  <h3>Is Gua a Godot or Unity Editor MCP?</h3><p>No; the roles are complementary. Editor tools operate the development environment, while Gua observes, operates, and verifies the runtime UI of a running Godot or Unity game.</p>
  <h3>Does Gua locate buttons with image recognition?</h3><p>No. Primary targeting uses IDs, roles, text, and state published by the game. Screenshots remain available as evidence for rendered output and visual comparison.</p>
  <h3>Does Gua replace the game engine or AI?</h3><p>No. Gua is the automation layer connecting a game runtime to tests, Inspector, development AI, and AI agent players.</p>
